    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The shape of the trick"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Most hash map solutions follow one template: sweep the array once, and at each element ask whether something you have **already recorded** completes the answer."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const seen = new Map();\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n  if (seen.has(whatWouldCompleteThis(arr[i]))) {\n    // found it\n  }\n  seen.set(arr[i], i);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The inner loop that would have searched for the partner is replaced by one lookup. O(N²) becomes O(N)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why lookup is constant time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The map runs the key through a **hash function** producing a number, and uses that number to index directly into an internal array. No scanning — it computes where the value should live, exactly like array indexing."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Collisions, where two keys hash to the same slot, are handled by keeping a small list per slot. With a decent hash function collisions are rare, which is why the guarantee is *average* O(1) rather than worst-case."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What it costs"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Space: O(N).** You are storing what you have seen. Two pointers uses O(1) — that is the trade.",
        "**Unordered.** A map tells you *whether* something exists, never what is largest or nearest.",
        "**Hashable keys only.** Numbers and strings are fine; arrays and objects need conversion to a stable string."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "When to reach for it"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When you find yourself writing an inner loop that searches for a value, stop and ask whether a map could remember it instead. That reflex is worth building — it is the single most reliable way to remove a nested loop."
    }
  ],
  "updatedAt": "2026-09-13T09:09:27.161Z"
}
